DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/race-and-gender-in-the-global
 -hispanophone-7911\n\nThe Race and Gender in the Global Hispanophone Resea
 rch Group is excited to invite you to a talk by Professor Heidi Tinsman (P
 rofessor of History at UC Irvine) on her forthcoming book\, Bound Brothers
 : Chinese Labor\, Masculinity\, and Nation in Nineteenth-Century Peru and 
 Chile. The talk will be on Monday\, November 16th from 4:00pm to 5:30pm in
  Pigott Hall\, Room 252. \n\nRSVP HERE FOR RACE AND GENDER IN THE GLOBAL H
 ISPANOPHONE EVENT\n\nAbstract: \n\nBound Brothers asks how Peruvian and Ch
 ilean nations were shaped by connections to China and the labor and agency
  of Chinese people within South America. Indentured Chinese workers brough
 t through the so-called “coolie trade” was vital to the capitalist exp
 ort economies that anchored South American republics and fueled armed conf
 lict between Peru and Chile during the War of the Pacific (1879-1883). Thi
 s book highlights the significance of gender\, especially masculinity\, to
  the politics of Chinese labor and nation-making in Peru and Chile. Men co
 mprised ninety-nine percent of Chinese people in nineteenth-century Peru a
 nd Chile. Why and how did this matter? The book traces Chinese men’s eve
 ryday connections to each other as well as to Chilean and Peruvian men. Th
 e term bound brothers refers to fraternal solidarity among men as well as 
 to Chinese bondage as indentured contract workers. Chinese men recreated a
 ll-male associations common in China to negotiate life in Peru and Chile. 
 Chinese sworn brotherhoods\, merchant guilds\, and mutual-aid s
